---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// V-678 — developer docs page for OAuth third-party app authors.
// Describes the V-667 family (registration, authorize, exchange,
// introspect, revoke) from the perspective of an integrator, NOT
// from the perspective of an internal Driftstack engineer.
//
// Posture: OAuth client registration is admin-gated. Integrators request a
// confidential client through support; the live flow requires client_secret
// plus PKCE-S256 and does not implement a public-client variant.

// W214.B — scope set matches the `api_key_scope` Postgres enum
// (apps/server/src/db/schema.ts). OAuth requests for any scope not
// in this enum are rejected with `invalid_scope` at /v1/oauth/authorize.
// `account_owner`, `gui_control`, the legacy `read`/`write`/`admin`
// aliases, and the `driftstack_internal_admin` scope are issued via
// the API-keys flow and are intentionally NOT exposed to OAuth clients.
const SCOPES = [
  { name: 'read:sessions', desc: 'Read session metadata + history.' },
  { name: 'write:sessions', desc: 'Start + stop sessions on the account.' },
  { name: 'read:profiles', desc: 'Read browser-profile metadata.' },
  { name: 'write:profiles', desc: 'Create + update browser profiles.' },
  { name: 'admin:profiles', desc: 'Reserved — no route enforces it; profile writes use write:profiles.' },
  { name: 'read:webhooks', desc: 'List webhook endpoints + delivery history.' },
  { name: 'write:webhooks', desc: 'Reserved — endpoint management requires account_owner.' },
  { name: 'admin:webhooks', desc: 'Reserved — secret rotation and deletion require account_owner.' },
  { name: 'read:api-keys', desc: 'List the account’s API keys (metadata only).' },
  { name: 'admin:api-keys', desc: 'Reserved — minting and revoking require account_owner.' },
  { name: 'read:billing', desc: 'Read invoices, usage, and crypto-order history.' },
  { name: 'admin:billing', desc: 'Initiate checkout sessions + manage subscriptions.' },
  { name: 'read:audit', desc: 'Read the account audit log.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="OAuth apps — Driftstack developer docs" description="Integrate a third-party app with Driftstack using OAuth 2.0 + PKCE-S256. Endpoints, scopes, token lifecycle, revocation.">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <h1>OAuth 2.0 integration</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      Driftstack uses OAuth 2.0 with PKCE-S256 for third-party app
      access. This page documents the authorization code flow your
      app uses to act on a Driftstack customer's behalf.
    </p>
    <p>
      Customer authorization requires an API-enabled paid tier, including any
      Manual tier. Free remains available for interactive desktop use, but
      cannot approve an OAuth authorization. Existing access tokens are
      rejected while an account is Free and resume after upgrade only if they
      have not expired or been revoked.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting a client_id</h2>
    <p>
      OAuth client registration is admin-gated. Email
      <a href="mailto:developers@driftstack.dev">developers@driftstack.dev</a>
      with:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your app name and a one-paragraph description</li>
      <li>Your redirect URI(s) — HTTPS only in production,
        <code>http://localhost:&lt;port&gt;</code> is accepted for development</li>
      <li>Which scopes you need (see the table below); we'll push back
        if the request is broader than your use case justifies</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We turn around new client registrations within one business
      day. You get back a <code>client_id</code> (prefixed
      <code>oac_</code>) and a <code>client_secret</code> (prefixed
      <code>oas_</code>). The secret is shown to you ONCE — we only
      store the hash on our side, so we cannot recover it for you.
      Treat it like a Stripe secret key.
    </p>

    <h2>Available scopes</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>What it grants</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {SCOPES.map((s) => (
          <tr><td><code>{s.name}</code></td><td>{s.desc}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Scopes follow the verb:resource pattern. Always request the
      narrowest scope set your app actually needs — broad scopes get
      pushed back during the human review step.
    </p>

    <h2>Authorization flow</h2>

    <h3>1. Mint a PKCE verifier</h3>
    <p>
      Generate a high-entropy random string (RFC 7636 §4.1, 43–128
      characters from <code>[A-Za-z0-9-._~]</code>). Compute the
      <code>code_challenge</code> as the URL-safe base64 of
      <code>SHA-256(verifier)</code>. Keep the verifier in your app
      until step 3 — never send it to anyone except Driftstack at
      the token endpoint.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Redirect the customer to Driftstack</h3>
    <pre><code>{`GET https://app.driftstack.dev/oauth/authorize/
  ?client_id=oac_…
  &redirect_uri=https://yourapp.com/oauth/callback
  &state=<csrf-token>
  &code_challenge=<sha256(verifier) base64url>
  &code_challenge_method=S256
  &scope=read:sessions write:sessions`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The customer signs into Driftstack if they aren't already, then
      sees the app label, requested scopes, and callback host before
      choosing Approve or Cancel. Approval returns them to your
      <code>redirect_uri</code> with <code>?code=…&amp;state=…</code>;
      cancellation returns <code>?error=access_denied&amp;state=…</code>.
      Verify <code>state</code> matches what you sent (CSRF guard) in
      either case.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-tk-ink-3">
      The Dashboard validates and stages this request through
      <code>GET /v1/oauth/authorize</code>, then performs approved consent
      through <code>POST /v1/oauth/authorize/complete</code>. Those are
      provider-internal steps: your integration never receives or handles
      the intermediate <code>authorization_id</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Exchange the code for an access token</h3>
    <pre><code>{`POST /v1/oauth/token
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "grant_type": "authorization_code",
  "code": "...",
  "code_verifier": "<your pkce verifier>",
  "client_id": "oac_…",
  "client_secret": "oas_…",
  "redirect_uri": "https://yourapp.com/oauth/callback"
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>The response carries the token:</p>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "access_token": "oat_…",
  "token_type": "Bearer",
  "expires_in": 3600,
  "scope": ["read:sessions", "write:sessions"]
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Tokens live for <strong>one hour</strong>. There are no
      refresh tokens — when the token expires, run the full
      authorize → exchange dance again. This is intentional: it
      makes consent re-confirmation a regular event rather than a
      forever-grant.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Use the token</h3>
    <p>
      Bearer-token authentication against the Driftstack API:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`Authorization: Bearer oat_…`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The token can only call API surfaces whose required scope is a
      subset of the token's scope. A token with
      <code>read:sessions</code> can call <code>GET /v1/sessions</code>
      but not <code>POST /v1/sessions</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Introspection (RFC 7662)</h2>
    <p>
      Check whether a token is still active + what scopes it carries:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`POST /v1/oauth/introspect
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "token": "oat_…",
  "client_id": "oac_…",
  "client_secret": "oas_…"
}

→ { "active": true, "client_id": "oac_…", "account_id": "acc_…",
    "scope": ["read:sessions"], "exp": 1736600000 }`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      After your client credentials are authenticated, a revoked,
      expired, unknown, or foreign-client token returns
      <code>{`{"active": false}`}</code>. Invalid or revoked client
      credentials return 401 before token lookup. Use introspection
      sparingly and only from server-side code — the request contains
      your <code>client_secret</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Revocation (RFC 7009)</h2>
    <p>
      Invalidate a token before its 1-hour expiry — useful when the
      customer signs out of your app:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`POST /v1/oauth/revoke
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "token": "oat_…",
  "client_id": "oac_…",
  "client_secret": "oas_…",
  "token_type_hint": "access_token"
}

→ 200 OK`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      After client authentication succeeds, the endpoint returns 200
      for an owned, unknown, or foreign-client token, but only a token
      issued to your <code>client_id</code> is revoked. This preserves
      RFC 7009 anti-enumeration behavior without allowing cross-client
      revocation. Invalid or revoked client credentials return 401.
      <code>token_type_hint</code> is optional and informational.
    </p>

    <h2>Security expectations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Always validate <code>state</code></strong> on the
        redirect back. Without it, your callback is wide open to CSRF.</li>
      <li><strong>Generate a fresh PKCE verifier per authorization
        flow.</strong> Re-using a verifier defeats its purpose.</li>
      <li><strong>HTTPS-only redirect_uri</strong> in production.
        Driftstack rejects HTTP redirect URIs that aren't localhost.</li>
      <li><strong>Store client_secret server-side.</strong> Every supported
        client is confidential and the token, introspection, and revocation
        endpoints require the secret. A browser UI must exchange through its
        own trusted backend; there is no public-client flow.</li>
      <li><strong>Tokens are bearer tokens.</strong> Anyone who reads
        the token can act as the customer. Treat them like passwords.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Rate limits</h2>
    <p>
      Token-issued requests count against the customer's account
      rate limits, not your app's. If you're going to mint many
      tokens for many customers, be aware that high per-customer
      QPS will hit the tier limits.
    </p>

    <h2>Sandbox</h2>
    <p>
      Driftstack has no separate sandbox environment. Test with a paid dev
      account on the production API and use least-privilege scopes. Read-only
      scopes cannot create sessions, but the account still uses production
      data, limits, and rate-limit budgets. The Free desktop
      <code>ds_test_…</code> credential is not an OAuth or API sandbox key.
    </p>

    <h2>Support</h2>
    <p>
      Questions, bug reports, or scope requests:
      <a href="mailto:developers@driftstack.dev">developers@driftstack.dev</a>.
      We aim to respond within one business day.
    </p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
